Genomic studies of Nigerian and African E. coli, and how public genome data have been used.
Genomic work on African E. coli has grown. It now includes multi-country and One Health surveillance. Even so, African genomes remain under-represented in global collections compared with the continent's burden of infectious disease (Lewis et al., 2023; Musicha et al., 2017). A systematic review of diarrhoeagenic E. coli (DEC) in Africa shows how narrow the evidence is. Of the underlying studies, 80% were hospital-based, 91% focused on children, and only 4.4% described outbreaks (Kalule et al., 2024).
4.1 Nigeria: clinical and carriage isolates
Most Nigerian genomic work comes from the south-west, especially Ibadan. In a study of 477 children under five in northern Ibadan, DEC carriage was common and the children's well water was contaminated (Akinlabi et al., 2023) and isolates from related case–control studies were later sequenced. Akinlabi et al. characterised 96 enteropathogenic E. coli (EPEC) isolates for virulence genes, serotypes and phylogeny. EPEC had rarely been typed molecularly in Nigeria before this (Akinlabi et al., 2025).
Among hospital isolates, Medugu et al. sequenced 107 multidrug-resistant E. coli from a tertiary hospital (2019–2020). Virulence genes were common, 81.3% of them were linked to mobile genetic elements, and virulence and resistance genes sat together on plasmids (Medugu et al., 2025). In the same isolates, fluoroquinolone resistance came mostly from chromosomal gyrA and parC mutations. Plasmid-mediated resistance contributed little (Medugu et al., 2026). A 2026 preprint extends this work to Ekiti and Ondo States and looks for signs of zoonotic, human-to-human and environmental transmission (Uzairue et al., 2026).
4.2 Nigeria: food, animal and environmental sources
A second group of studies follows E. coli along the food chain. Aworh et al. examined ESBL-producing E. coli in abattoir workers, beef cattle and abattoir environments in Abuja and Lagos. Their findings were consistent with transmission from animals to people along the beef chain (Aworh et al., 2022). In Kwara State, sequencing of ESBL-producing isolates from poultry showed multidrug resistance together with mobile elements, virulence genes and phages (Al-Mustapha et al., 2023). Fish have also been sampled. Isolates from catfish caught in the River Niger in Anambra State were characterised by MLST and cgMLST (Ekwuazi et al., 2024). A single poultry-associated isolate from Enugu State carried resistance and virulence genes on both the chromosome and plasmids (Ejikeugwu et al., 2026).
4.3 Genomic studies elsewhere in Africahttps://github.com/Nigerian-Pathogen-Genomics-Group/nigeria-ecoli-amr-genes/tree/main/docs
Outside Nigeria, Malawi has the deepest record. Musicha et al. described the genomic landscape of ESBL-producing E. coli in Blantyre, including its clade structure and blaCTX-M carriage (Musicha et al., 2017). Later, Lewis et al. sequenced 473 colonising ESBL-producing E. coli from adults in Blantyre. The dominant global sequence types (ST131, ST410, ST167) and blaCTX-M genes matched worldwide patterns. However, 37% of isolates fell outside well-described lineages (Lewis et al., 2023). That diversity was only visible because the sequencing was done locally.
In West Africa, Yehouenou et al. sequenced 19 ESBL-positive E. coli from surgical site infections in hospital patients in Benin, a small collection placed in wider phylogenetic context using publicly available genomes (Yehouenou et al., 2021)).
Multi-country studies show how resistance spreads. Ingle et al. studied atypical EPEC from children in seven countries, four of them in sub-Saharan Africa. About 65% of isolates were resistant to three or more drug classes, whether or not the child had diarrhoea. Genotype explained over 95% of phenotypic resistance (Ingle et al., 2018). The BARNARDS network sequenced resistant Gram-negative bacteria causing neonatal sepsis in seven African and South Asian countries, including Nigerian sites. E. coli was only part of that picture (Sands et al., 2021). In Madagascar, the WHO Tricycle protocol was used to track ESBL-producing E. coli in humans, chickens and the environment (Milenkov et al., 2024). Other countries are being encouraged to follow this model. A study across four African low- and middle-income countries combined metagenomics and whole-genome sequencing to characterise enteric pathogens, including E. coli, from human, animal and environmental sources. It focused on rural communities that surveillance often misses (Thystrup et al., 2025). In South Africa, sequencing has described the population structure and resistance of avian pathogenic E. coli from poultry (Wang et al., 2026).
4.4 How public genome data have been used
Placing new isolates in context. Two Nigerian studies did this. Akinlabi et al. set their locally derived EPEC genomes beside international EPEC genomes, and the Nigerian and international sets are deposited together as a dataset (Akinlabi et al., 2025). Afolayan et al. sequenced 68 bloodstream isolates from three sentinel hospitals (2016–2018) and deposited the reads in ENA, with assemblies in Pathogenwatch and a Microreact project. They also checked their data against public sequences. A clade of 11 ST10/ST167 isolates carried an O-antigen gene cluster nearly identical to one from an avian outbreak strain in China, and a capsular gene 99.6% identical to one from Klebsiella variicola (Afolayan et al., 2022). Outside Nigeria, Lewis et al. compared their 473 Malawian genomes with the curated collection of 10,146 human E. coli genomes that Horesh et al. built from public data, to see how much of the local diversity was new (Horesh et al., 2021; Lewis et al., 2023). Only 246 of those 10,146 genomes were from Africa, so apparent Malawian clades may partly reflect a lack of sampling elsewhere on the continent (Lewis et al., 2023). In Benin, Yehouenou et al. did the same at small scale (Yehouenou et al., 2021).
Re-analysing repository assemblies. Malesa et al. analysed 159 ST10 genomes from 19 African countries, of which only four were newly sequenced and 155 were already public. More than 71% were multidrug resistant, 29% carried blaCTX-M-15, and most clusters were human-derived. The data pointed to both local evolution and long-distance spread (Malesa et al., 2026). Matsumura et al. also relied on public data, using 2,905 genomes to study the global evolution of ST131 clade A (Matsumura et al., 2026). Gichure et al. combined 11 new DEC genomes from Maputo with 125 public DEC assemblies from Southern African Development Community countries, taken from EnteroBase. They used these to link clades to geography, niche and pathovar (Gichure et al., 2025). Shawa et al. took 295 chromosomal sequences of African Enterobacteriaceae from GenBank and predicted chromosomally integrated blaCTX-M genes in silico. They produced no new sequence data (Shawa et al., 2025).
Reusing isolate collections for new questions. This is well shown in the Ibadan work. Isolates collected for earlier diarrhoea case–control studies were later sequenced and used for EPEC epidemiology (Akinlabi et al., 2025), so one collection answered more than one question.


